From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v0lumehi <aljic.zijad@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:01:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] NSP script: remove the shader libraries of older chain
 versions after a successful build

Every change to the shader chain translates all shaders again into a new b-<chain> folder, and nothing removed the old ones: a Breath of the Wild build folder held b-157e1b96c8da (2.1 GB) and b-a78f966a7b38 (2.3 GB) besides the current one, and each upstream update added another.

build_shaders now calls prune_stale_chains last, after the libraries, their tables, the regression check and the reuse key of the current chain are in place (a reused build prunes too). It removes the b-<12 hex digits> folders of other chains in shader/cache and shader/ios and their keys (ios/b-<chain>.key, mac-b-<chain>.key), and logs one line with the freed size. A build that stops or fails earlier keeps the old libraries. Nothing else reads an old folder: the host and the packers take only the current chain, shader_regressions.py compares with the next chain's predecessor, which is the folder that stays, and the Metal compile cache already took the old libraries in. The compile cache is not touched.

Tests in test_nsp_mac.py: two old chains are removed and the current one stays, a failed build and a build without libraries keep everything, other names (cas, a file, a malformed name) stay. Reference entry in docs/reference/shaders.md, the Breath of the Wild note and the symptom index.

Upstream-Status: forwarded seedds/nxnative#682
Reason: a build folder grows by about 2 GB per game with each upstream chain change until upstream merges it
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#231

